A batch of small, unrelated cleanup items found while reviewing #94 (email verification) — each
too minor to justify its own file, so they're tracked together here. Unlike the grouped-file
convention for a single staged initiative, these entries aren't ordered or dependent on each
other; they're just being scheduled and shipped together.

---

## Reject blank Brevo/config secrets, not just missing ones

### Status
Done.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`Program.cs` and `BrevoEmailSender.cs` both guard required configuration values with
`config[...] ?? throw ...`, which only catches a missing key — an empty string passes through
silently.

### Motivation
`??` only fires on `null`. If `Brevo:ApiKey` or `Brevo:SenderEmail` is set to `""` (e.g. an
unset environment variable exported blank, or a config layer overriding it with an empty value),
the app starts up normally instead of failing fast, then sends every Brevo request with a blank
`api-key` header or a blank "from" address. Since `BrevoEmailSender.SendEmailAsync` swallows send
failures as a logged warning rather than throwing, this fails completely silently in
production — with `RequireConfirmedAccount = true`, every new registrant would be permanently
stuck waiting for a confirmation email that never arrives, with nothing but a log line to explain
why.

### Proposed Changes
Replace both `?? throw` checks with a blank-string-aware guard (e.g.
`string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(...)` before assignment, throwing the same
`InvalidOperationException` message) in:
- `WhenWorksWeb/Program.cs`'s `Brevo:ApiKey` read.
- `WhenWorksWeb/Services/BrevoEmailSender.cs`'s `Brevo:SenderEmail` read.

### Acceptance Criteria
- An empty-string `Brevo:ApiKey` throws at startup instead of silently starting the app.
- An empty-string `Brevo:SenderEmail` throws at `BrevoEmailSender` construction instead of
  silently sending mail with a blank "from" address.
- A missing (`null`) value for either still throws exactly as it does today.

### Out of Scope
- `Authentication:Google:ClientId`/`ClientSecret` in `Program.cs` — not reported as having this
  gap; revisit separately if desired.

