## Harden and simplify BrevoEmailSenderTests

### Status
Done.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`BrevoEmailSenderTests.cs` is missing coverage for an empty-string `Brevo:SenderEmail`
(only `null` is tested, so the fix above wouldn't be caught by regression if it slipped), and
defines three near-identical private `HttpMessageHandler` subclasses that differ only in what
their `SendAsync` override does.

### Motivation
`Constructor_WithoutSenderEmailConfigured_Throws` only exercises the `null` case, leaving the
blank-string gap (see the fail-fast fix above) untested. Separately, `StubHttpMessageHandler`,
`ThrowingHttpMessageHandler`, and `TimingOutHttpMessageHandler` each exist solely to return a
canned response, throw `HttpRequestException`, or throw `TaskCanceledException` from the same
single overridden method — a future fourth failure mode currently means writing a fourth whole
class instead of one more lambda.

### Proposed Changes
1. Add a test case constructing `BrevoEmailSender` with `Brevo:SenderEmail` set to `""`,
   asserting it throws the same way the `null` case does.
2. Collapse the three `HttpMessageHandler` subclasses into one reusable
   `DelegateHttpMessageHandler(Func<HttpRequestMessage, CancellationToken, Task<HttpResponseMessage>> handler)`,
   with each existing test passing the appropriate lambda instead of a dedicated class.

### Acceptance Criteria
- An empty-string `Brevo:SenderEmail` is covered by a test asserting construction throws.
- The three single-purpose `HttpMessageHandler` subclasses are replaced by one delegate-based
  handler; all existing test cases pass using it.

### Out of Scope
- Any change to `BrevoEmailSender`'s production code beyond what the fail-fast fix above already
  covers.
